WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:06.630
Hey youtubers it’s charlie.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:10.290
Doctor Who Week Continues with my list of
top 5 Doctors.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.650
My friends and I have been doing a bunch of
special videos to celebrate the 50th anniversary

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.090
this week so be sure to subscribe to get everything.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:20.520
We’re even doing a special livestream and
I’ve also got some special news about something

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.340
I’m doing on the BBC America television
channel.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.800
So let’s get started, my number 5 favorite
doctor - Peter Davison the fifth doctor.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:30.849
So he had the unfortunate task of following
up Tom Baker who’s also on this list.

00:00:30.849 --> 00:00:34.390
In order to differentiate himself his Doctor
became this really charming and sensitive

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:35.390
person.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:40.090
Think of it as being like the jump from the
more mature Tennant to a slightly less silly

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:41.090
Matt Smith.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:47.079
His doctor was also the first to directly
oppose violence and always came up with much

00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:48.370
more creative ways to solve problems.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:53.261
io9 actually ranked his final episode the
Caves of Androzani as the greatest doctor

00:00:53.261 --> 00:00:57.050
who story of all time.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:59.640
Number 4 - Matt Smith, the eleventh doctor.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:06.360
So just like Peter Davison, Smith had to follow
an incredibly popular doctor who is also on

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:07.360
this list.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.820
He really won me over with his boundless enthusiasm.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:12.460
All of time and space, where do you want to
go.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:16.430
And with Stephen moffat at the helm they introduced
a lot of really new aspects of the doctor’s

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:17.430
life that we’d never seen before.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:20.380
Namely River Song, the fields of trenzalore
and now the john hurt doctor.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:24.700
Smith’s run also helped popularize doctor
who in the united states in a way that hadn’t

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:25.700
been done before.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.970
Previously Doctor Who had a strong cult following
here, but now the show reaches a much larger

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:29.970
audience.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:33.210
My Favorite Matt Smith episode is definitely
the doctor’s wife. because he may have married

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:34.210
river song.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:37.840
But he married the tardis first or the tardis
married him.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.170
depending on how you look at it.

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.730
Number 3 - Christopher eccleston, the Ninth
doctor.

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:47.580
He’s most famous for regenerating Doctor
Who in such a new and complex way.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:52.840
His Doctor was damaged by war and had to live
with the guilt of essentially dooming his

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.480
entire race to time prison.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:02:00.090
But he also had this fun adventurous spark
and it was so amazing to watch him jump back

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.890
and forth between those extremes.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.090
Of all the doctors, he was also one of the
most imposing.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:08.979
According to River Song the Doctor is the
most fearsome man in all of time and space.

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:17.260
And eccleston had this largeness and intensity
that really made you believe it.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:21.489
My favorite Christopher Eccleston episode
was Rose, because that was the episode that

00:02:21.489 --> 00:02:24.019
brought the series back.

00:02:24.019 --> 00:02:26.230
Number 2 - Tom Baker, the fourth Doctor.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:30.250
Even though Four had the benefit of being
on the classic show at the height of it’s

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:32.879
popularity, there were so many good writers
and the best companion the show has ever had.

00:02:32.879 --> 00:02:33.879
And then there is the scarf.

00:02:33.879 --> 00:02:34.879
If you’ve ever been to a cosplay convention
then you’ve seen the scarf.

00:02:34.879 --> 00:02:36.329
Even if you’re not familiar with doctor
who you recognize the scarf.

00:02:36.329 --> 00:02:40.760
Baker had this really goofy but witty sense
about him that made you feel like the doctor

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:46.560
was taking himself far less seriously than
during the third doctor’s more action-heroey

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:47.890
run.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:03:04.129
Without it feeling like 
a parody of itself.

00:03:04.129 --> 00:03:15.529
Part of me feels like some of that comedy
survived the regeneration into Matt Smith’s

00:03:15.529 --> 00:03:16.569
doctor.

00:03:16.569 --> 00:03:19.140
My favorite Tom Baker episode is City Of Death.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:26.240
It was written by Douglas Adams under the
name David Agnew which itself became a legend

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:27.240
of doctor who.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:32.730
The episode was one of the great classic who
epics where the style, the story and the cast

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.749
come together in the best possible way.

00:03:34.749 --> 00:03:36.310
And my number 1 Doctor.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:38.760
David Tennant, the 10th doctor.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:42.980
So many things changed about the character
and the show in general during Tennant’s

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:46.239
run that I feel like he deserves his spot
here.

00:03:46.239 --> 00:03:49.670
Just to give you an idea of how much he grew
doctor who’s audience, the BBC had to shorten

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:50.670
their broadcast window.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.609
That’s the time between when an episode
airs and when it airs in other countries.

00:03:53.609 --> 00:03:58.859
Because so many people in international countries
were downloading it The BBC fundamentally

00:03:58.859 --> 00:04:01.530
altered the way in which they broadcast the
show around the world.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:09.730
This week for instance the BBC is airing the
50th anniversary episode in every country

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:12.989
at the exact same moment for this very reason.

00:04:12.989 --> 00:04:17.150
Tennant had this amazing commitment to the
role that allowed him to go from this lighthearted

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:20.440
sweet man and turn on a dime to All powerful.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:23.539
“He’s like fire and ice and rage.” that
came from tennant.

00:04:23.539 --> 00:04:25.880
My favorite David Tennant episode is Blink.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:28.050
Because weeping angels.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:32.870
The idea that a stone statue could get you
when you’re not looking at it.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:36.900
And a girl getting help from an alien time
traveler who’s leaving her clues in the

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:39.940
form of DVD easter eggs.

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:43.340
Stephen Moffat also won two awards for the
story.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:54.180
All I can say is that you need to buy the
DVD of the 50th Anniversary episode and watch.all.of.the.extras.for.clues.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:58.650
But since everyone experiences Doctor Who
in their own way let me know who your top

00:04:58.650 --> 00:04:59.812
5 doctors are in the comments below.

